

PS(1)               UNIX Programmer's Manual                  PS(1)



NAME
     ps - process status

SYNOPSIS
     ps

DESCRIPTION
     _P_s prints one line per process, a synthetic line for the
     kernel itself (pid 0), and a summary of the bank-0 stack
     arena.  It takes no options: FGX has no /dev/kmem and no
     proc filesystem, so the kernel hands the whole table over
     through one system call and _p_s formats it.

     The columns are:

     PID    process id.

     PPID   parent process id.

     ST     state.  R runnable or running, S sleeping, T
            stopped, Z zombie (exited, not yet reaped).

     STK    pages of bank-0 stack RESERVED for this process,
            guard page included.  This is what consumes the
            arena: a process gets what its binary's header asks
            for, plus a page for the argv/envp frame, plus the
            guard.

     HWM    bytes of that reservation the process has actually
            TOUCHED at its deepest so far.  This is a
            measurement, not an estimate: the kernel fills every
            stack it carves with a known word, and _p_s asks it
            for the lowest address where that word is gone.  A
            dash means the region lies outside the arena and was
            never filled.

     HEAP   bytes of C heap this process had left at its
            TIGHTEST -- again a measurement, not an estimate.
            The heap is the tail of the process's 64 KB data
            bank, grown by sbrk(2), and the kernel keeps a
            high-water mark of the break alongside the break
            itself.  The peak rather than the current figure,
            because a process that peaks while it starts up and
            then frees reads as idle afterwards.  A dash means
            the process has no data bank of its own.

     CMD    command name.

     The last line reports the stack arena as a whole, counted
     from the allocator's own page bitmap rather than summed
     from the rows above it:

           stack arena: 25/147 pages used

     Bank-0 stack is the scarcest thing on the machine -- every
     task's stack must live in bank 0 because the 65816 stack
     pointer is 16 bits -- so STK and HWM are the columns to
     read when a spawn fails for want of room.  HEAP is the
     other end of the same trade: moving a large local off the
     stack puts it in the data bank, where it comes out of this
     number.

DIAGNOSTICS
     A process whose HWM approaches its reservation is close to
     its guard page; entering the guard page is logged by the
     kernel and can be read with dmesg(1) ("GUARD stkhwm").  A
     process whose reservation is many times its HWM is holding
     arena that nothing else can use.

BUGS
     Every process is listed; there is no way to select, sort or
     widen the output.

     The high-water mark understates by however much a program
     happens to write the fill word into its own deepest frame.

SEE ALSO
     dmesg(1), kill(1), vmstat(1)

